        <!-- Article Body -->
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">A double-conversion UPS does not draw current as a sine wave. It pulls short, steep pulses at the crest of the voltage, and the Fourier series of that pulse train is the fundamental plus the 5th, 7th, 11th, 13th and higher orders. In an AI data center the rectifier front end runs near full load continuously, so that pulse train becomes a permanent harmonic current on the bus. The result is <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">THDi</strong> well above the <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">IEEE 519</strong> planning limit, extra transformer heating, and neutral current nobody sized for. This article covers why UPS systems cause harmonics, what they cost at the PCC, and how a <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">300 A active harmonic filter</strong> delivers UPS harmonic mitigation at the source.</p>
<h2 id="how-ups-harmonics-are-quantified" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">How UPS Harmonics Are Quantified</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">A six-pulse rectifier produces characteristic orders at 6n ± 1 — the 5th, 7th, 11th, 13th and so on, with amplitude falling roughly as 1/h. A twelve-pulse or active-front-end rectifier shifts the spectrum but does not remove it.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">THDi% = √(Σ I_h²) / I_1 × 100%</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">THDv% = √(Σ V_h²) / V_1 × 100%</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6"><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">IEEE 519</strong> sets the current-distortion limit at the PCC by the ratio of short-circuit current to load current (I_SC/I_L). At a typical data-center bus with I_SC/I_L between 20 and 50, the limit for orders below 11 is <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">7.0%</strong>, for the 11th–16th range <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">3.5%</strong>, and total demand distortion <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">8.0%</strong>. A modern UPS at 40–50% load commonly measures <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">25–35% THDi</strong> without filtering.</p>
<h2 id="major-causes" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Major Causes</h2>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Rectifier front ends</strong> — the dominant source; a 6-pulse thyristor or diode bridge is the worst case.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Lightly loaded UPS modules</strong> — distortion in percent rises as the module unloads, because the fundamental falls while the pulse current stays steep.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Switched-mode IT load</strong> — server PSUs and PDUs add their own 3rd and 5th content, and triplen orders accumulate in the neutral.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Parallel UPS configurations</strong> — circulating current between modules adds interharmonic content.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Cooling and auxiliary VFDs</strong> — chillers and CRAH fans contribute 5th/7th distortion on the same bus.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Weak source impedance</strong> — a generator-backed or long-fed bus amplifies voltage distortion for the same current spectrum.</li></ul>
<h2 id="what-it-costs" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">What It Costs</h2>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Transformer derating</strong> — harmonic current raises eddy and stray losses; a K-rated or oversized unit is often the only passive answer, and it is expensive.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Neutral overheating</strong> — triplen orders sum rather than cancel; a neutral sized for balanced load carries real current.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">UPS efficiency loss</strong> — distortion raises rms current for the same kW, so I²R losses climb across cables and switchgear.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Generator interaction</strong> — harmonic current into a standby generator raises voltage distortion and can trip the transfer sequence during a test.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Capacity stranded</strong> — apparent power consumed by distortion cannot be sold to IT load, so the facility runs out of headroom before it runs out of kW.</li></ul>
<h2 id="why-fixed-methods-fall-short" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Why Fixed Methods Fall Short</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">A detuned passive filter is tuned to one order and is blind to the rest. A line reactor reduces distortion at the drive terminals but not at the PCC. A capacitor bank corrects average displacement power factor but cannot track a UPS whose load steps from 30% to 90% in seconds — and it risks resonance with the upstream transformer.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">None of these methods respond to the actual <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">data center power quality issues</strong>: a load that changes shape every rack refresh, and a harmonic spectrum that shifts with UPS firmware and module count.</p>
<h2 id="how-a-300-a-ahf-fixes-ups-harmonic-mitigation" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">How a 300 A AHF Fixes UPS Harmonic Mitigation</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">A <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">CHITEK AHF</strong> samples load current through CTs, computes the harmonic content each cycle, and injects a compensating current of opposite phase through a voltage-source inverter.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">i_c* = i_L − i_s(fundamental)</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">The inverter switches at high frequency on IGBTs and tracks the reference within <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">&lt; 1 ms</strong>, pulling <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">THDi from 30%+ down to &lt; 5%</strong> at the injection point. Filtering, reactive compensation and phase balancing can share one enclosure.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Sizing follows measured load, not transformer nameplate. A worked example, with assumptions stated:</p>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li>Measured UPS input current at full IT load: <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">400 A rms</strong>.</li><li>Measured THDi: <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">30%</strong>, giving harmonic current of about <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">120 A rms</strong>.</li><li>Target THDi at the PCC: <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">5%</strong>, leaving about <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">20 A</strong> of residual harmonic.</li><li>Required compensation: 120 − 20 = <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">100 A</strong>, plus <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">15–25%</strong> headroom for load growth and spectrum shift.</li></ul>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">That lands inside a <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">300 A</strong> frame with margin for a second UPS module or a future AI rack row. The <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">harmonic filter 300a data center</strong> rating is chosen for worst-case load, not average.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">CT placement decides whether the device works at all. The CTs must sense load current on the load side of the correction point; if they see already-corrected current, the controller reads zero error and idles.</p>
<h2 id="igbt-vs-sic" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">IGBT vs SiC</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Conventional LV AHF units use IGBTs capped near <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">150 °C</strong>, switching typically below <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">20 kHz</strong>. SiC MOSFETs raise the junction limit to <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">200 °C</strong>, carry roughly <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">10×</strong> the breakdown field, switch at <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">2–3×</strong> the frequency, and cut switching loss by about <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">50%</strong>. The wider control bandwidth tracks the fast load steps of an AI hall more cleanly and shrinks the cabinet footprint — the direction CHITEK's LV AHF platform is built on.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6"><table class="w-full border-collapse"><thead><tr class="bg-gray-50"><th class="text-left p-4 font-semibold text-text-dark border-b border-border-light">Criterion</th><th class="text-left p-4 font-semibold text-text-dark border-b border-border-light">IGBT AHF</th><th class="text-left p-4 font-semibold text-text-dark border-b border-border-light">SiC AHF</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Junction limit</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">~150 °C</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">~200 °C</td></tr><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Switching frequency</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">&lt; 20 kHz</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">2–3× higher</td></tr><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Switching loss</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">baseline</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">~50% lower</td></tr><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Control bandwidth</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">adequate</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">wider</td></tr><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Cabinet size</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">larger</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">smaller</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="engineering-deployment-checklist" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Engineering Deployment Checklist</h2>
<ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li>Measure phase currents, THDi, individual orders and THDv at the PCC over a full duty cycle, including UPS module transitions.</li><li>Record I_SC at the PCC to confirm the <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">IEEE 519</strong> limit that applies.</li><li>Place the AHF at the UPS input board, close to the rectifier cluster.</li><li>Verify CT orientation and location before energizing; confirm the controller sees load current.</li><li>Audit existing capacitor banks for resonance and mistuning.</li><li>Size from measured harmonic current plus <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">15–25%</strong> headroom.</li><li>Re-measure after commissioning against the IEEE 519 limit and record the residual spectrum.</li></ol>
<h2 id="typical-application-profiles" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Typical Application Profiles</h2>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">AI training halls</strong> — dense GPU racks with high, steady UPS loading; a 300 A AHF holds THDi below 5% at the input board.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Colocation and multi-tenant sites</strong> — mixed UPS vintages and IT load; the AHF adapts as tenants change.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Edge and modular data centers</strong> — a single AHF enclosure protects a shared bus with limited space.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Generator-backed facilities</strong> — filtering before the transfer point reduces voltage distortion during generator tests.</li></ul>
<h2 id="bottom-line" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Bottom Line</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">UPS harmonic mitigation is a current-cancellation problem, not a capacitor problem, and in an AI data center the load is too dynamic for fixed methods. A <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">300 A active harmonic filter</strong> at the UPS input board restores <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">IEEE 519</strong> compliance and releases transformer capacity. CHITEK's AHF and SVG platforms are specified from measured harmonic current, not nameplate ratings. What is your measured THDi at the UPS input — and how much headroom does your transformer have left?</p>
